WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.980
The next thing we're going to cover is serialization and saving games.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.019
Whilst this sounds like a pretty substantial topic in terms of you on your game,

3
00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:14.099
normally the same system can get quite sizable, depending on the kind of game you're making,

4
00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:20.940
but actually in terms of what you need to get a save system implemented in Unreal is actually pretty limited.

5
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.820
First off, what actually is save games, I think most of the people will know this,

6
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.059
but it gives us the ability to save the state that we define of the game,

7
00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:35.579
close the game, reboot it, and players are able to resume from where they last left off.

8
00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:39.100
The implementation is very much down to the kind of game that you're making.

9
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.820
If you're making a roguelike, you may not try and save the actual active world at the moment,

10
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.939
but maybe you save the unlocks that they have.

11
00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:51.259
If you're saving something like a huge open-world RPG,

12
00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:55.659
maybe you need to save where they are, the AIs around them, and it gets much more complicated.

13
00:00:55.659 --> 00:00:59.899
In Unreal, really what it provides us is the ability to save games to a file

14
00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:04.299
and load them back in, and then what we do beyond that is kind of up to us.

15
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:10.219
You can have multiple forms of save class, which is useful even within a single project,

16
00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:15.019
because a very typical implementation might have you having a settings save file,

17
00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:19.739
which is kind of global to all users, and then a per user save file.

18
00:01:19.739 --> 00:01:24.700
For player one, you have got three slots and you have three different saves

19
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.890
that you can save in each of those independently.

20
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.129
As well as that, obviously it supports multiple save game files, like I already mentioned,

21
00:01:32.129 --> 00:01:34.769
and the save game isn't restricted to C++.

22
00:01:34.769 --> 00:01:39.969
You can have a Blueprint-only project and you can still implement all of this functionality.

23
00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:45.090
So there's actual steps for having a project that actually has a save game

24
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.769
and you can save it and load it.

25
00:01:46.769 --> 00:01:51.730
First thing you need to do is actually make your save game class, which inherits from save game.

26
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.290
Then you just choose what data that's going to store,

27
00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:00.290
then you really need to define two key pieces of data, really one, which is your save slot name.

28
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.170
It depends on the platform you're on.

29
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.969
Save game index can or cannot be relevant.

30
00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:11.490
Actually by default on Windows, the save game, the user index it's called,

31
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.169
actually isn't relevant, but it can apply for other platforms.

32
00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:20.210
Then we just use the create save game object, create that save object,

33
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.969
put the data that we want to save into that object, and then save it.

34
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.090
We can do that synchronously or asynchronously.

35
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.490
Let's actually just quickly go through a demonstration of this.

36
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.849
In our example project, I've just made a class for us to reference.

37
00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:36.849
It's as simple as this.

38
00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:40.289
You make a Blueprint that inherits from the save game class.

39
00:02:40.289 --> 00:02:42.990
Blueprint, save game.

40
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.300
It's that simple.

41
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.460
The one I've already made, very easy.

42
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.300
It inherits from the save game and I've just got this one float in it,

43
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.139
which is just some float that we want to save.

44
00:02:56.189 --> 00:02:59.229
What can be a little bit confusing is you'll see in Unreal,

45
00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:02.349
when you have a variable selected and you go into the advanced section,

46
00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:04.270
we have this save game flag.

47
00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:08.110
This doesn't magically make this get saved.

48
00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.349
It's not really doing a huge amount for it.

49
00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:12.590
It's not going to put it into your save file.

50
00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.509
In fact, this save data will save even if that isn't ticked.

51
00:03:16.509 --> 00:03:22.189
What this save game actually does is marking it for this variable for serialization.

52
00:03:22.189 --> 00:03:25.469
When you're trying to save a U object or a struct,

53
00:03:25.469 --> 00:03:28.189
and you're trying to serialize that object or struct

54
00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:30.590
down into something that can be written into your save file,

55
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.830
that is what that flag is there to mark.

56
00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.310
It's to say that when I try and serialize this object,

57
00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.030
I'm interested, this piece of data is relevant.

58
00:03:40.509 --> 00:03:42.669
This is like a serialization helper that just means

59
00:03:42.669 --> 00:03:46.189
you don't have to manually put in every single variable for each class.

60
00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.389
Unreal will find it for you to say,

61
00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:51.789
oh, this is relevant to saving and loading.

62
00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:55.229
If you mark this off, it's more about object serialization.

63
00:03:56.479 --> 00:03:59.680
Either way, we've got this variable in our save game.

64
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.840
If I went to our level blueprint,

65
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.919
I've just done a little bit of a setup here.

66
00:04:03.919 --> 00:04:05.680
Essentially, on our begin play,

67
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.199
we're going to try loading two different save files.

68
00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:13.599
As I said, on Windows, the user index isn't relevant.

69
00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:16.560
In all of these, I've just left user index is zero.

70
00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.800
To define the two different save files,

71
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.360
instead, I've got this, I've actually customized the save slot name.

72
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.360
So we've got some default name, which is just example save slot.

73
00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.180
And then we are just appending a value to it.

74
00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.420
So this is to represent multiple save slots.

75
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.379
So the name plus one and the name plus two.

76
00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:42.139
And we're trying to load both of those.

77
00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:46.300
If it loads, we read out the value, that some save float value,

78
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.660
and we'll just print that out and set that as our save game.

79
00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:51.579
And we do the same thing for number two,

80
00:04:51.579 --> 00:04:54.300
but print out that it was from number two and set that as save two.

81
00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:58.430
And then I've just got this, I'm just binding to these button keys.

82
00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.550
When I press one, we randomize that number,

83
00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.670
print out what the number was,

84
00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.870
and save that game with that value written into it.

85
00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.629
And same for two, but just for our second slot.

86
00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:12.509
So if I, I'm just going to clear out my save files.

87
00:05:13.709 --> 00:05:16.589
So to kind of demonstrate this, I'll come back to this in a second.

88
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:25.779
But if I go and play a game, you see no save game found for one or two.

89
00:05:25.779 --> 00:05:29.540
Now if I hit one, that says it's saving the value is 3.8.

90
00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.100
Now if we just remember that, if I stop, quit play in editor and play again,

91
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.180
you'll say it's found found no save game two,

92
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.819
but save game one, the value saved is 3.8.

93
00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:41.860
And so if I do two, it's now 4.8.

94
00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.220
And if I regenerate one, so the save file already exists,

95
00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.819
but I'm just overwriting that data.

96
00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:51.220
So 4.8 and 9.4, if I stop and play again,

97
00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.899
we'll see 4.8 and 9.4 in those two different slots.

98
00:05:54.899 --> 00:05:55.939
So it's that simple.

99
00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:57.540
You just put data in the save object,

100
00:05:58.339 --> 00:06:01.220
you create the save object, you push that data into it,

101
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.259
and then you save it.

102
00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:04.980
And yeah, the load functions work in the same way.

103
00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.449
So the only other thing is about where this data is actually saved.

104
00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:10.930
You might have just, if you were paying close attention,

105
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.490
then you might have noticed me jumping into a folder there

106
00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.329
just to delete the previous saves.

107
00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:19.250
You can see by default, when you're in an editor project,

108
00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.410
it will get whatever project you're in.

109
00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:26.050
So in this case, example five, five project in the saved folder,

110
00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.170
you can see we've got a save games folder,

111
00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:30.689
and that's where they'll get created.

112
00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:40.129
And these names will just match whatever you're feeding into that save game function call.

113
00:06:40.129 --> 00:06:44.050
So just this slot name is what that literally the name of that file will be.

114
00:06:45.329 --> 00:06:47.649
That being said, on a fully built version of your game,

115
00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:51.490
it won't necessarily be, I think in a development build,

116
00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.730
it will be next to your executable.

117
00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.329
But in a shipping version of your game,

118
00:06:55.329 --> 00:06:58.290
it will actually go into AppData on Windows at least.

119
00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.009
I can't comment about Mac, but just worth bearing that in mind.

120
00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:04.209
If you're struggling to find your direct people to your save games

121
00:07:04.209 --> 00:07:06.050
in a shipping build of the game,

122
00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:10.050
it's probably somewhere in your AppData, like AppData local,

123
00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.810
whatever your game name is, something like that.

124
00:07:12.769 --> 00:07:17.810
But that's essentially all you need to do to get a project saving.

125
00:07:18.689 --> 00:07:20.449
The kind of last thing just to mention

126
00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:22.930
is the fact that there are a couple of these key functions.

127
00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.569
We've already covered some of them, creating the save game object,

128
00:07:25.569 --> 00:07:27.810
saving the games to slot and loading the games to slot.

129
00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:31.329
But there are also asynchronous variations of these things.

130
00:07:31.329 --> 00:07:33.889
Both of these are available in blueprint and C++.

131
00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.610
And it is despite the fact that the demonstration I just gave

132
00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.930
did actually utilize synchronous saving and loading just for the sake of speed.

133
00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.290
It is obviously strongly encouraged

134
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.930
that you probably lean on the asynchronous functionality,

135
00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:51.889
especially if your game has larger save slots for loading and saving,

136
00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:54.610
just because otherwise your game is going to freeze up and hitch.

137
00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.930
And obviously that's not desirable.

138
00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.410
So the asynchronous versions of these functions

139
00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:00.449
are the ones that we encourage,

140
00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:02.610
but otherwise it works in exactly the same way.
